  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "$\\frac{\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}}=$",
  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $2-\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $2+\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $2+\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}} & =\\frac{\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{2+\\text{i}}{2+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\text{i}+{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2\\text{i}}{{2}^{2}-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2\\text{i}}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2\\text{i}}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1+2\\text{i}}{5}=-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
